WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.360
- [Mark] Okay. All right, J.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.193
- Yes.

00:00:07.193 --> 00:00:08.026
- [Mark] J, where'd you grow up?

00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:08.859
Where're you from originally?

00:00:08.859 --> 00:00:10.160
- [J] Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.440
- [Mark] And tell me about your childhood.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.850
You had both your parents
when you were young?

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.002
- [J] Yeah, they were both there.

00:00:15.002 --> 00:00:17.873
My dad eventually left
when I was about 12.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.387
- [Mark] And how would you
describe your childhood?

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.393
- It was tumultuous.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Very, I don't know,
dysfunctional and my parents

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.320
were both kind of like
absent most of the time.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.430
Like, I was just kind of on my own, really

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.560
for the most part, so.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:40.980
- [Mark] No abuse or anything like that?

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.180
- Yeah, there was a lot
of verbal, some physical.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.000
They were both alcoholics, so yeah.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.160
- [Mark] You had siblings?

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.453
- Yes. Two older sisters, indeed.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.830
- [Mark] And you, you finished school?

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:56.880
- I did finish high school, yeah.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.353
- [Mark] So what'd you do after that?

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.170
- Not much. I had drank for a
few years and then I ended up

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.400
just getting on a Greyhound and
coming out to San Francisco.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.200
- [Mark] So it's been,
you've been out here since?

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.130
- 2000, 2002. Yeah.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.560
- [Mark] And what did
you do when you got here?

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.890
- Well, I continued to drink
for a little while and I moved

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.310
out here with my best friend at the time

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.500
and she ended up going into treatment

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.500
and I kind of just
graduated into narcotics

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.750
and basically just, you know,

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.493
partying and running
around the Tenderloin.

00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:40.990
- [Mark] And what is your,
what is your drug now?

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:45.763
- My drug now? Fentanyl and crack cocaine.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.703
- [Mark] And you're, you're living where?

00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:54.493
- In a hotel on Lombard, with my friend.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.510
- [Mark] So you're supporting
yourself the way a lot

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.070
of the other girls will
support themselves here?

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.290
I'm assuming.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:04.900
- Well, I actually will
try to do any hustle to

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.020
avoid actually doing any sex work

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:12.020
but I do a lot of delivery
for methamphetamine.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.180
And then I'm also a doctor,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:22.180
which is I assist people
in injecting drugs safely.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:23.403
- [Mark] Is that right?

00:02:23.403 --> 00:02:27.550
- Yes, I do it.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.230
They, you know, or they
know I'm responsible

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.410
for them being able to get high.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:34.380
So they pretty much take
care of me pretty well.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.603
So, it works out good.

00:02:37.395 --> 00:02:38.228
Yeah.

00:02:38.228 --> 00:02:40.710
- [Mark] Do you still have
contact with your family?

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:41.697
- I do. I do, yeah.

00:02:41.697 --> 00:02:42.700
- [Mark] And they know what you're doing

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.080
or where you're at?

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.940
- Yeah, I'm pretty open and honest

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.310
about everything, so yeah, they know.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.290
- [Mark] What's their reaction?

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.283
- They worry a lot.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.333
Try to encourage me to come home.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:03.927
I don't know, they just yeah,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:10.970
I cause them a lot of worry.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.023
Yeah, unfortunately.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.060
I try to reassure them that,

00:03:26.078 --> 00:03:29.853
I'm a lot safer than a
lot of people, I think.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.520
I don't do anything to reckless

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.440
or I don't put myself in
any compromising situations

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.863
as long as I can help it.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.827
Yeah. I don't know.

00:03:42.827 --> 00:03:45.003
- [Mark] And drug use for
you started at what age?

00:03:45.976 --> 00:03:48.030
Before you came out to San Francisco?

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:53.030
- Yeah, I was 16 when I first
started doing narcotics.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.150
- [Mark] Who introduced you to them?

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.550
- My dad always had pills and stuff around

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:08.820
and I just knew it was known
that he like, you know,

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.440
he was a pill popper.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.940
So, you know, like I would
eventually start stealing them

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.453
from him until he started
giving them to me.

00:04:19.357 --> 00:04:21.450
- [Mark] And your
parents are both addicts?

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.283
- Yes.

00:04:22.283 --> 00:04:23.833
- [Mark] And are they still using today?

00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:25.573
- My mom isn't. My dad is.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:31.430
- [Mark] And looking back at
your life, do you have regrets?

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:32.808
Any decisions you made

00:04:32.808 --> 00:04:35.933
that you wish you hadn't
or anything like that?

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:37.910
- Yeah, definitely.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.860
I regret not being more present

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.883
for my nieces and nephews and growing up.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:57.070
I regret not, I don't know

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.980
just taking more opportunities
and just kind of going

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:09.060
with my gut more instead of
denying myself all the time

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.610
and talking myself out
of things a lot. Yeah.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.108
- [Mark] Have you been in love before?

00:05:15.108 --> 00:05:16.920
- Yeah. Yes, I have.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.060
- [Mark] Can you tell me about that?

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.320
- Oh, it's (laughing) the most amazing,

00:05:27.961 --> 00:05:32.961
beautiful, indescribable
experience I've ever had

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:39.140
and also the most devastating and painful.

00:05:43.405 --> 00:05:47.072
- [Mark] What was that,
just the breakup or?

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.063
- I think the breakup was a relief.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.710
That was a better part,
I think, towards the end,

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:59.290
but no just having to face yourself

00:06:04.942 --> 00:06:08.760
and having to face all
the ugly things, you know,

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.890
about yourself as well
as the beautiful things.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.820
And, also having to accept
that about the person you love

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.720
and having to remember in
the end that you're both

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.563
just human beings, you know?

00:06:22.859 --> 00:06:24.690
And we both make mistakes

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:29.419
and nothing is forever and
nobody's perfect, you know?

00:06:30.952 --> 00:06:33.802
- [Mark] What's the most
important lesson you've learned?

00:06:45.703 --> 00:06:48.536
- [J] That only I am in control of

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:58.831
whether I'm, you know,
in pain or feel good

00:06:58.831 --> 00:07:01.840
or only I'm in control of being hurt

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.723
and dealing with some negativity.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.753
I only let,

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:17.763
I only allow,

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:31.273
that's my responsibility
of what goes on in my life

00:07:32.467 --> 00:07:33.730
and what I'm affected by

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:38.230
and what I choose to be a part of

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:41.409
and what I let happen to me.

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:43.530
It was under my control only, so yeah.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:46.617
- [Mark] Do you feel like
the fact that your parents,

00:07:46.617 --> 00:07:50.040
like you grew up with
parents that were addicts,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.000
may have opened up the door
for you to do the same?

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.310
I mean, it sounds like you're raiding

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:56.910
your dad's pills early.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:57.743
- [J] Yeah.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:05.170
It definitely made it easier,
more prevalent, I think.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:10.483
I would've probably either
anyway, anyway I would've,

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.400
you know, became an
addict myself regardless.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:17.533
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.753
And living this life where,

00:08:25.254 --> 00:08:27.480
you're on the Tenderloin and there's lots

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.460
of drugs and crime and prostitution

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.070
and all that kind of stuff
going on all around you.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:34.410
Is that, does that get depressing for you?

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:36.803
Does it get on your nerves after a while?

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.463
- Extremely, yeah.

00:08:40.533 --> 00:08:42.869
It's a small, small little area with

00:08:42.869 --> 00:08:47.869
a large amount of negative,
concentrated negative energy

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:52.540
so it's constantly overwhelming and it's-

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:53.900
- [Mark] You get depressed sometimes?

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.993
- All the time, yeah.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:07.570
I don't know, I remain disconnected

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.870
as much as possible and try to be

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.050
not present as much as possible, so.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.430
- [Mark] Yeah. What do
you think the solution

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:18.280
might be for you?

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:30.543
- Discipline.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:35.513
I don't know.

00:09:35.513 --> 00:09:37.800
- [Mark] Do you think
loving yourself could be

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.073
a part of the solution?

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:41.768
- Yeah.

00:09:41.768 --> 00:09:43.230
- [Mark] Sometimes the shame that you feel

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.570
and stuff like that
gets, you know, sabotage,

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:48.570
you know it just creates self-sabotages.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:54.510
- It makes it hard to have a,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:58.093
somewhat normal, functional
human interaction or,

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:04.320
you know, it makes interpersonal
relationships difficult,

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:05.870
for sure.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:06.890
- [Mark] And do you have kids?

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:07.953
- No, I don't.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.410
- [Mark] I thought maybe
you were transgender

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:13.243
when I first met you because-

00:10:13.243 --> 00:10:14.076
- I am.

00:10:14.076 --> 00:10:15.070
- [Mark] You are? You are?

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:15.903
- I am.

00:10:15.903 --> 00:10:16.736
- [Mark] Oh, I didn't know.

00:10:16.736 --> 00:10:17.569
I was always wondering.

00:10:17.569 --> 00:10:18.402
I thought maybe you might be.

00:10:18.402 --> 00:10:19.860
- Yeah, I am.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:20.910
- [Mark] Oh, you are?

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.010
You had me fooled.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:27.810
- God's got a weird sense of
humor, I suppose, I guess.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.380
- [Mark] And was that part of
the problem with your family?

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:32.213
- No.

00:10:32.213 --> 00:10:33.060
- [Mark] Did they take it well?

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.070
- Yeah, surprisingly. I
was really lucky, yeah.

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.503
Compared to most people so.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:41.470
Pretty supportive family and yeah.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:42.303
- [Mark] That's great.

00:10:42.303 --> 00:10:43.136
- Yeah.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.730
- [Mark] And being a transgender woman,

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:49.413
that makes it tougher out here, now?

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:55.690
- I don't feel like anything's
more difficult or anything

00:10:55.690 --> 00:11:00.310
is, you know, harder than
it is for anybody else.

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:03.673
You know, I don't buy
into that whole, you know?

00:11:07.259 --> 00:11:09.301
- [Mark] You don't buy into it?

00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:10.630
- I don't, I don't.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.543
I feel like, you know,

00:11:15.683 --> 00:11:18.373
it's just, you know, a different scenario,

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:22.610
different details, Same (beep), you know?

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:25.530
- [Mark] What was the
best part of your life?

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.423
Best memories? Best time?

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:30.223
- Good question.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.960
Probably the three years I was married.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:42.993
To my husband Isaac, yeah.
Best time of my life.

00:11:44.483 --> 00:11:47.120
- [Mark] How many years ago was that?

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:47.953
- Six.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.570
- [Mark] And how did that fall apart?

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:59.140
- He was a really paranoid schizophrenic

00:12:06.169 --> 00:12:07.750
and we were taking a lot
of stimulants as well,

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:12.590
so that made it a million times worse.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.721
So he ended up taking his own life.

00:12:14.721 --> 00:12:15.585
- [Mark] Oh, he did?

00:12:15.585 --> 00:12:16.418
- Yeah.

00:12:16.418 --> 00:12:17.251
- [Mark] I'm sorry.

00:12:17.251 --> 00:12:18.893
- It's okay. It's not your fault.

00:12:21.827 --> 00:12:23.670
It's part of life.

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:25.010
Death is a part of life.

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:29.960
- [Mark] What do you look
forward to for your future?

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:32.659
Do you have hopes of doing anything?

00:12:32.659 --> 00:12:35.890
- Backpacking through
Europe, studying history.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:37.669
- [Mark] History, yeah.

00:12:37.669 --> 00:12:42.650
- [J] Yeah. That's my skeleton plan.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:43.483
- [Mark] That's great.

00:12:43.483 --> 00:12:44.877
- Yeah. I'm excited.

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:47.603
I'm looking forward to it.

00:12:47.603 --> 00:12:49.500
- [Mark] Cool. Well J, thank you so much

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.610
for sharing your story.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:51.443
- [J] Yeah.

00:12:51.443 --> 00:12:52.276
- [Mark] I wish you the best of luck

00:12:52.276 --> 00:12:53.760
and hope you make it to Europe.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:54.593
- [J] Thank you.

00:12:54.593 --> 00:12:55.426
- [Mark] All right. Thank you.

00:12:55.426 --> 00:12:56.259
- [J] Thanks.

